INSERT INTO `paper_submission` VALUES (19, 'Study on the Expression and Correlation of PCBP1 in Tears of Dry Eye Induced by  Sjögren\'s Syndrome.', 'Shengjia Hu', '15158820864', '387914040@qq.com', 'Objective To study the expression level of PCBP1 in tears of patients with dry eye induced by primary Sjögren\'s syndrome （PSS） and the correlation between which and the clinical dry eye indexes. Methods  Compared  the indexs between  groups of normal, Non-SS DED and SS-DED.Conclusion PCBP1 was highly expressed in tears of patients with dry eye induced by PSS and was strong correlated with OSDI score, TMH, NIBUT（f）, NIBUT（av）, MGL, Sit, and the index of eye redness analysis.', 'Primary Sjögren\'s syndrome; Dry eye; PCBP1', 'https://paper-submission-prod.oss-cn-shanghai.aliyuncs.com/speakers/avatars/2026-07-14/9dfad2d1b7ec4e409be692087e9ac426.docx', NULL, '1', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 'Shengjia Hu', NULL, '0');
INSERT INTO `paper_submission` VALUES (22, 'Myopia Control Efficacy of HORI Lenses in Children: A Multicenter Study Using a Bayesian Framework with Meta-Analytic-Predictive Priors', 'Zhirong Zhang', '17323950489', 'zrzhang37@163.com', 'Purpose: To evaluate the efficacy of myopia control in children and adolescents wearing Hexagon Optimized Reticular Intergration (HORI) spectacle lenses compared with historical single-vision (SV) lenses using a Bayesian framework.\nDesign: A multicenter retrospective study combined with a meta-analysis.\nMethods: Historical SV lenses data was retrieved from a meta-analysis, which was conducted to identify all RCTs up to 18 April 2026 that participants fitted with standard SV lenses. PubMed, Embase, Web of Science, Cochrane, and ClinicalTrials.gov were systematically searched. A Bayesian framework then combined the meta‑analytic prior with individual data from 9 centers’ retrospective cohort fitted with HORI lenses. The main outcome measures were the 1-year axial length (AL) elongation and 1-year SE change.\nResults: Of 162 studies, 25 were included (N=1820). The pooled estimate for 1‑year AL change was 0.32 mm (95% CI: 0.29 to 0.35, I² = 95%), and the SE progression was -0.64 D (95% CI: -0.71 to -0.57, I² = 94%) in participants wearing SV lenses. To reduce heterogeneity, priors restricted to Chinese studies (17 studies, N=1185). In the HORI group (295 eyes), the annual AL change was 0.15 ± 0.14 mm and SE change was -0.28 ± 0.33 D. Posterior probability of AL reduction was 100%. Probabilities of achieving reductions ≥ 0.10, ≥ 0.15 and ≥ 0.20 mm/year were 99.80%, 90.45% and 37.70% respectively. The probabilities of achieving SE slowing of ≥ 0.25 D/year was 87.75%.\nConclusions: This study provides externally benchmarked Bayesian evidence consistent with slower progression among HORI lens users compared with historical SV controls.', '', 'https://paper-submission-prod.oss-cn-shanghai.aliyuncs.com/speakers/avatars/2026-07-21/a8a16313f7c4472a821f39cf5a43e2fd.docx', NULL, '1', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 'Zhirong Zhang', NULL, '0');
INSERT INTO `paper_submission` VALUES (30, ' Influence of Baseline Myopia Degree on Myopia Control Efficacy of Orthokeratology Lenses', 'GE,YUNXIN', '18753501760', '2587826880@QQ.COM', 'Objective: To analyze the influence of baseline myopia on the myopia control effect of orthokeratology lenses. Methods: This retrospective study recruited 177 patients (354 eyes) newly fitted with orthokeratology lenses in the Ophthalmology Department of Yantai Zhifu Hospital from January 2019 to January 2023. Subjects were categorized into four groups based on initial spherical equivalent (SE): Group A (SE ≤ -1.50 DS, 13 patients, 26 eyes), Group B (-1.50 DS ＜ SE ≤ -3.25 DS, 109 patients, 217 eyes), Group C (-3.25 DS ＜ SE ≤ -4.75 DS, 41 patients, 81 eyes), Group D (-4.75 DS ＜ SE ≤ -6.00 DS, 15 patients, 30 eyes). Uncorrected visual acuity, cycloplegic refraction, intraocular pressure and corneal parameters were examined before lens wear and 2 years after wear (1 month after lens discontinuation). Complication rates were recorded, and intergroup myopia progression was compared. Results: No intergroup differences were observed in uncorrected visual acuity, intraocular pressure and complication incidence (P＞0.001). Significant differences were found in corneal curvature change and spherical equivalent shift after lens cessation (P＜0.001). Myopia progression in Group A was markedly greater than Groups B, C and D (P＜0.001), while Groups B, C and D showed no statistical discrepancy (P＞0.001). All groups maintained low complication risks. Conclusion: Orthokeratology lenses provide effective myopia control for children with initial myopia exceeding 150 degrees, featuring low complication incidence and reliable safety.', ' myopia control,orthokeratology lenses,baseline myopia degree', 'https://paper-submission-prod.oss-cn-shanghai.aliyuncs.com/speakers/avatars/2026-07-29/7ec51dce993144aeb76035538b9851f0.docx', NULL, '1', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 'GE,YUNXIN', NULL, '0');
INSERT INTO `paper_submission` VALUES (34, 'A Physics-Informed Prediction Model for CRT Lens Parameters Based on Corneal Sagittal Height: A Retrospective Study of 219 Clinical Fitting Prescriptions ', 'Zhixuan Huang', '13982297593', 'kai8xuan@126.com', 'Abstract\nObjective： To analyze the quantitative relationships between corneal refractive therapy (CRT) lens parameters and patients’ ocular biometric parameters, and to develop prediction models for lens parameters.\nMethods： This was a retrospective study. Prescription data from 219 eyes of patients successfully fitted with CRT lenses were collected. Spherical power, cylindrical power, flat keratometry (Kf), steep keratometry (Ks), horizontal visible iris diameter (HVID), and baseline axial length were set as independent variables, while base curve (BC), return zone depths (RZD1, RZD2), landing zone angles (LZA1, LZA2), and lens diameter (Dia) were set as dependent variables. Multiple linear regression, random forest regression, and a physics-informed hybrid algorithm based on corneal sagittal height theory were adopted for modeling. Model performance was assessed using 5-fold cross-validation and external validation with 33 independent cases.\nResults： The BC flattening magnitude was strongly correlated with spherical power (r=−0.946). The simplified formula BC = 1.092 × rKf(flat K curvature radius) − 0.207 × spherical power − 0.520 yielded a cross-validated R² of 0.946, and 95.9% of prediction errors were within ± 0.1 mm. RZD1 was correlated with Kf (r=0.658), increased RZD2 was associated with corneal astigmatism (P<0.001), and lens diameter (Dia) was correlated with HVID (r=0.605). The hybrid algorithm exhibited optimal predictive performance for RZD2 (R²=0.616). External validation showed that the BC formula reached an R² of 0.953, with a 100% agreement rate within ± 0.1 mm. For RZD2 prediction, the hybrid algorithm yielded the lowest MAE (12.1 μm) and the highest exact agreement rate (55%) among all models. The within-one-step agreement rates of all parameters ranged from 94% to 100%.\nConclusions： There are definite quantitative relationships between CRT lens parameters and patients’ routine ocular biometric parameters. The prediction models achieve high accuracy for BC. The physics-informed hybrid algorithm further improves the predictive performance of RZD2, with favorable generalizability verified by external validation. It can serve as a quantitative reference for selecting parameters of the first trial lens.\n\n', 'Key words orthokeratology, corneal refractive therapy, lens parameters, prediction model, machine learning; myopia', 'https://paper-submission-prod.oss-cn-shanghai.aliyuncs.com/speakers/avatars/2026-07-31/a9a82c15c77647a8954eff6dd3fe8e36.pdf', NULL, '1', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 'Zhixuan Huang', NULL, '0');
INSERT INTO `paper_submission` VALUES (36, 'ceshi', 'ceshi', '18921901290', 'maomao060214@gmail.com', '11111111111111111111111111', '11111', '', NULL, '1', NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 2, 'ceshi', NULL, '0');
INSERT INTO `paper_submission` VALUES (37, 'Microstructural Alterations of the Cornea and Limbus after Scleral Lens Therapy for Severe Dry Eye: An in vivo Confocal Microscopic Study', 'Lu Chuwei', '19858732824', 'lcw19858732824@163.com', 'Purpose: To evaluate the microstructural alterations of the cornea and limbus after scleral lens (SL) therapy in patients with severe dry eye (DE) using in vivo confocal microscopy (IVCM).\nMethods: This prospective cohort study enrolled 25 eyes of 25 patients with severe DE. Ocular surface symptoms and clinical signs were assessed at baseline and after 1, 3, and 6 months following SL therapy. IVCM was performed at each visit to analyze longitudinal changes of subbasal dendritic cell (DC) density, basal epithelial cell density (BECD), and endothelial cell density (ECD) at the central corneal  as well as limbal BECD. The morphological alterations of the palisades of Vogt (PoV) were also assessed.\nResults: After SL therapy, both symptoms and clinical signs of DE improved significantly. The Ocular Surface Disease Index scores and corneal fluorescein staining scores decreased remarkably, while fluorescein tear break-up time significantly increased accompanied with the improvement in vision-related quality of life. Analyses on IVCM images revealed that compared with baseline, the infiltration of subbasal DCs at the central cornea decreased significantly, which presented as a pronounced reduction of DC density at 1 month after SL therapy (p = 0.0419) and maintained a relatively low level thereafter. No significant changes were found in central corneal BECD, ECD and limbal BECD over the entire study period (all p > 0.05). Although the morphology of PoV predominantly appeared to be an atypical pattern, no statistically significant differences were identified with the elongation of follow-up.\nConclusions: Short- to medium-term SL therapy could effectively alleviate DE symptoms and signs in this Chinese cohort, with a significant reduction of subbasal DC infiltration at the central cornea. Notably, the microstructures of the cornea and limbus remained stable at the cellular level within 6-month SL wear.', 'dry eye; scleral lens; in vivo confocal microscopy; dendritic cells; basal epithelial cells', '', NULL, '1', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 'Lu Chuwei', NULL, '0');
